","question_type":"application","question":"Which coefficient sequence gives $1+x+\\frac{x^2}{2}+\\frac{x^3}{6}+\\cdots$ as a power series centered at $0$?","options":["$c_n=n!$","$c_n=\\frac{1}{n!}$","$c_n=n$","$c_n=0$ for all $n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0059344db0a09b96e570d4e2","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_power_series_f8394d94_009_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the formal expression $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} c_n(x-a)^n$ not by itself tell you for which $x$ the power series converges?","options":["Every expression of this form converges for all real $x$.","The definition fixes only the form; whether the series converges depends on the $c_n$ and on $|x-a|$.","Every expression of this form diverges at every point $x\\neq a$ unless all of its coefficients $c_n$ are equal to zero.","A power series is only defined once its sum is known in closed form."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1546f65affad24bcb8b1eefa","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_power_series_f8394d94_011_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Write the polynomial $2+3x$ as a power series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}c_nx^n$ centred at $0$. Which coefficient sequence is correct?","options":["$c_0=3$, $c_1=2$, and $c_n=0$ for all $n\\ge2$","$c_n=2+3n$ for all $n$","$c_0=0$, $c_1=2$, and $c_n=3$ for all $n\\ge2$","$c_0=2$, $c_1=3$, and $c_n=0$ for all $n\\ge2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c33d30bc193f2fcd87ce29e0","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_power_series_f8394d94_fill220261112_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The series $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(2x-6)^n}{n}$ is to be written in the form $\\sum_{n}c_n(x-a)^n$. What are its centre $a$ and its coefficients $c_n$ for $n\\ge1$?","options":["Centre $a=6$ and $c_n=\\frac{1}{n}$","Centre $a=3$ and $c_n=\\frac{1}{n}$","Centre $a=3$ and $c_n=\\frac{2}{n}$","Centre $a=3$ and $c_n=\\frac{2^n}{n}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d6d636131d9ef7a4f41d226","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_power_series_f8394d94_fill220261112_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"The power series $x-\\frac{x^3}{6}+\\frac{x^5}{120}-\\cdots$ is centred at $0$ and contains only odd powers of $x$. Writing it as $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}c_nx^n$, what are $c_2$ and $c_3$?","options":["$c_2=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $c_3=\\frac{1}{120}$","$c_2=0$ and $c_3=-\\frac{1}{6}$","$c_2=0$ and $c_3=\\frac{1}{6}$","$c_3=-\\frac{1}{6}$, while $c_2$ is undefined because $x^2$ does not occur"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d713590b4963ac9627625073","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_power_series_f8394d94_fill220261112_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $x\\in(-1,1)$ the power series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}x^n$ has sum $\\frac{1}{1-x}$, and $\\frac{1}{1-x}$ is also defined at $x=-2$, where it equals $\\frac{1}{3}$. What happens to the series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-2)^n$?","options":["It converges to $\\frac{1}{3}$, the value of $\\frac{1}{1-x}$ at $x=-2$","It converges, because its terms alternate in sign","It diverges, because its terms $(-2)^n$ do not tend to $0$","It diverges to $+\\infty$, because its partial sums keep increasing"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2f5de3e6572a31d8cb11326c","time_limit":45}]}}